**How to install and run:**

Open your spreadsheet, go to **Extensions → Apps Script**, delete anything in the editor, paste the script, and save (Ctrl+S). Close the Apps Script tab, reload the spreadsheet, and a new **Volunteer Tools** menu will appear in the menu bar within a few seconds. Click **Volunteer Tools → Clean Survey Data**. The first run will ask you to authorize the script.

**What it does, step by step:**

It reads every row from "Raw" without touching it, then loads your "ParkMap" lookup table. Each row gets normalised: `JoinedBefore` is mapped to Yes/No/REVIEW, phone numbers are stripped to digits (dropping a leading country-code 1) and flagged REVIEW if they aren't exactly 10 digits, timestamps are parsed from Date objects or common string formats, and `PreferredPark` is lowercased and stripped of non-letters to look up against ParkMap.

After normalisation, it makes two duplicate passes. The first groups rows by email and flags every row that shares an email with another. The second groups by name and flags any pair whose parsed timestamps fall within 10 minutes of each other. Then a superseded pass keeps only the most recent row per email group and marks the rest SUPERSEDED.

Finally, any row with at least one REVIEW field, any duplicate flag, a SUPERSEDED marker, or a blank email that name-collides with another row goes to the "Review" sheet. Everything else goes to "Clean". Both sheets are created (or overwritten) with the original six columns plus eight helper/status columns, and you get an alert with the counts.

**A few things to watch for:** The ParkMap sheet should not have a header row, or if it does, make the first row's column-A value something that won't collide with real data (e.g., "key" maps to lowercase "key" which won't match any park). The 7-digit phone numbers in your sample data will all land in Review since no formula or script can recover a missing area code — you'll need to fix those by hand or pull the area code from another source.

